eater haste than the family could handle. Adding the (few) family members killed for showing knowledge of koldunism, and those (several) killed by their own stupidity in human courts, the Ruthven-line began to dwindle. During the assault on the haven of the antediluvian a great portion of the remaining family were killed. The disorganized remnants, feeling they'd failed in their sacred duty, sought safety \u2013 and was absorbed \u2013 into the larger revenant families. <b>Disciplines</b>: I'm thinking Dominate here, maybe Auspex too. Koldunic Sorcery might be too extreme, since not even kolduns have it in-clan... <b>Weakness</b>: Maybe something along with the human sin of pride? Or bound to the soil in some way? Or Dark Faith hehe... Thoughts? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div> Awesome research work and very good writing! As for discipline suggestions, i'd go with Auspex, Animalism and Vicissitude. It seems the Ruthvens are very close to the tzimisce and the eldest, i think it would make sense to mirror them so much. Weakness, i don't know. Maybe something related to the idea of inbreeding or being raised too inhuman? The eerie presence flaw comes to mind. On the other Hand, it could make sense to suggest the Ruthvens were incorporated be the Basarab, the revenant family that actually birthed Vlad Tepes. They could even be one and the same. Their disciplines were Dominate, Protean and Vicissitude and their weakness a severe allergy to garlic that transferred even to undeath.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-18T09:24:04-08:00","id":32468,"title":"","content":"Thank you fellas, glad you liked it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I was loosely thinking that perhaps the Zantosa could be the family the Ruthven revenants was asorbed into. That could explain, in some sense, how the Zantosa turned from spies into more focused on how to wield power among humans. But that was just a thought...until I read what Valis wrote:<br /> Cause I had totally forgot about the Basarab! They not mentioned in either Guide to the Sabbat, CB: Tzimisce or the Dark Ages Vampire-material, that I read through most recently. Maybe the Dark Ages Vampire writers forgot about hem as well <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> Rereading Transylvania by Night, the Basarab and how I envisioned the Ruthven family have many similarities. A difference that I see, is how in-adept many of the Ruthven is at what is expected of them. I really like the &quot;spoiled children with weak wills and weaker genes&quot;-angle from CotI. I'll give it some more thought about how I would handle it - all suggestions welcome!<br /> The original Tzimisce disciplines makes a lot of sense, and something I considered myself. The reason I thought about including Dominate is that bad leaders with the means to impose the poor decisions upon their fellow men appeals to me. <br /> I really like your suggestions for the weakness as well Valis!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-19T10:43:46-08:00","id":33301,"title":"","content":"Actually, the idea of the Ruthven becoming a branch of the Zantosa is indeed very good, as it connects with the idea of protecting the Antediluvian's rest place. It could actually be argued that the Zantosa took the place of the Basarab as the Tzimisce's most prominent family, who in turn took the position from the Ruthven. One could say one family was absorbed into another as well, not exactly being replaced, but merged into the other and losing the surname. <br /> <br /> <br /> Another take on the inbreeding comes from the Duschesky, who betrayed the Tzimisce and defected to the Tremere. They became inbred under the Tremere, and their weakness reflects this: they cannot have Social Attributes higher than 2.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-23T10:22:56-08:00","id":35652,"title":"","content":"Hey All! Nice topic!<br /> <br /> I wish I could find my Ruthvenski revenant family notes to properly add to this discussion. I planned on posted them to my Dark Ages Vampire campaign website a while back but never quite finished it. If I recall correctly, I said that they were the oldest of the remaining revenant families (as of AD 1203, when my chronicle is set), based on something that I read somewhere that eventually a ghoul family will stagnate and eventually go sterile from inbreeding and excessive mutation. They were at one point preeminent among the revenant families but they had lost a lot of political ground to the Basarab and Szantovitch families since the absorption of Dacia into the Roman empire. I didn't have plans to portray them as a weak or fallen bloodline (I see Lambach as an aberration), but rather as a particularly insular bloodline more interested in preserving clan genealogies and history, as well as secretly protecting the torpid form of the Eldest (in keeping with their portrayal in the Dark Ages Clan Novel Tzimisce).<br /> <br /> Even so, a number of potent elder revenants still serve the formidable Lord Ruthven (who I place as the youngest of the Antediluvian's progeny), the most noted genealogist and historian among the clan and secretly the sworn protector of the Eldest. He is a Voivode of great power, who rules through his childer Damek and Tabak and on through their descendants. Lambach I have placed in the 6th generation, and he journeys throughout the Voivodate as the envoy of Lord Ruthven. He is obsequious in the extreme but welcome in most demesnes owing to his charm and lineage. At this point his family think him good for little else other than cataloguing the ever-changing power structures of the clan for his master (which ties in with the Eldest's plans for him down the road as a kind of clan chronicler). I wasn't too worried about generations here. The Anarch Revolt will provide plenty of opportunity for diablerie!<br /> <br /> I had the Ruthven &quot;Clan&quot; Disciplines as Auspex, Dominate and Vicissitude and their Family Flaw as Can't Cross Running Water. Auspex to represent their guardian role and scholarly duties. Dominate to represent their role as ancient lords. And Vicissitude for fairly obvious reasons such as their extreme longevity as a Tzimisce bloodline and their proximity to the eldest. I liked Can't Cross Running Water for a couple of reasons. Firstly, it made them more insular, in keeping with my themes for the family. Secondly, I was inspired by the folkloric roots of the Basarab weakness, and liked the idea of giving another ancient family a similar curse. Now that you mention the Bogatyr link I have a third reason: they might have some residual aversion to water over the Great Flood and the deal they had to make with the Eldest?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://www.obsidianportal.com/campaigns/the-concord-of-ashes\" target=\"_blank\">The Concord of Ashes</a>, my Dark Ages Vampire Chronicle","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1790-haligaunt","name":"Haligaunt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1790_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-23T11:05:31-08:00","id":35679,"title":"","content":"I'm a big supporter of the Lambach being 4th generation. I believe that was the intention of the many revised books. I posted before that those mentions of Lambach being 5th on revised were written by the same guy (Dean Shomshak), and also if it were up to him, the NPC section of Lair of the Hidden would never mention a Ruthven as the progenitor of such line (thanks Sarah or Janet for that!).<br /> <br /> -Saga","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","name":"Saga","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-26T01:53:24-08:00","id":37235,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>valismedsen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=33301#post33301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, the idea of the Ruthven becoming a branch of the Zantosa is indeed very good, as it connects with the idea of protecting the Antediluvian's rest place. It could actually be argued that the Zantosa took the place of the Basarab as the Tzimisce's most prominent family, who in turn took the position from the Ruthven. One could say one family was absorbed into another as well, not exactly being replaced, but merged into the other and losing the surname.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Ruthven --&gt; Basarab --&gt; Zantosa could be very cool. I'll have to roughly check some dates though. I'll get back on that <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Haligaunt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=35652#post35652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey All! Nice topic!<br /> <br /> I wish I could find my Ruthvenski revenant family notes to properly add to this discussion. I planned on posted them to my Dark Ages Vampire campaign website a while back but never quite finished it. If I recall correctly, I said that they were the oldest of the remaining revenant families (as of AD 1203, when my chronicle is set), based on something that I read somewhere that eventually a ghoul family will stagnate and eventually go sterile from inbreeding and excessive mutation. They were at one point preeminent among the revenant families but they had lost a lot of political ground to the Basarab and Szantovitch families since the absorption of Dacia into the Roman empire. I didn't have plans to portray them as a weak or fallen bloodline (I see Lambach as an aberration), but rather as a particularly insular bloodline more interested in preserving clan genealogies and history, as well as secretly protecting the torpid form of the Eldest (in keeping with their portrayal in the Dark Ages Clan Novel Tzimisce).<br /> <br /> Even so, a number of potent elder revenants still serve the formidable Lord Ruthven (who I place as the youngest of the Antediluvian's progeny), the most noted genealogist and historian among the clan and secretly the sworn protector of the Eldest. He is a Voivode of great power, who rules through his childer Damek and Tabak and on through their descendants. Lambach I have placed in the 6th generation, and he journeys throughout the Voivodate as the envoy of Lord Ruthven. He is obsequious in the extreme but welcome in most demesnes owing to his charm and lineage. At this point his family think him good for little else other than cataloguing the ever-changing power structures of the clan for his master (which ties in with the Eldest's plans for him down the road as a kind of clan chronicler). I wasn't too worried about generations here. The Anarch Revolt will provide plenty of opportunity for diablerie!<br /> <br /> I had the Ruthven &quot;Clan&quot; Disciplines as Auspex, Dominate and Vicissitude and their Family Flaw as Can't Cross Running Water. Auspex to represent their guardian role and scholarly duties. Dominate to represent their role as ancient lords. And Vicissitude for fairly obvious reasons such as their extreme longevity as a Tzimisce bloodline and their proximity to the eldest. I liked Can't Cross Running Water for a couple of reasons. Firstly, it made them more insular, in keeping with my themes for the family. Secondly, I was inspired by the folkloric roots of the Basarab weakness, and liked the idea of giving another ancient family a similar curse. Now that you mention the Bogatyr link I have a third reason: they might have some residual aversion to water over the Great Flood and the deal they had to make with the Eldest?</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool that we had some similar ideas!<br /> The genealogy angle is really good and fitting. Damek Ruthven had the most complete genealogical archive among his Clan, as you say according to <i>Dark Ages Novel: Tz</i>, and there were some servants creeping around in there that might have been revenants. Thing is, I don't want them to be a mix of all the other revenant families. It might be to much Obertus territory there, with some Zantosa and Basarab already thrown in. Any ideas how to handle it?<br /> The progenitor of the line - &quot;Ruthven&quot; - I kind of figured were ash, with the emphasis on Damek as the &quot;the eldest surviving descendant of the bogatyr lineages&quot;-line. But I have obviously not been able to come to a conclusion about how to handle just what that line means <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I'll think I'll go with Dominate too, but keeping with the Tzimisce Displines of Auspex and Vicissitude. Dominate is not unheard of among the Tzimisce either, with the Old Clan.<br /> And you made a strong case for the Can't cross running water-flaw!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saga</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=35679#post35679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a big supporter of the Lambach being 4th generation. I believe that was the intention of the many revised books. I posted before that those mentions of Lambach being 5th on revised were written by the same guy (Dean Shomshak), and also if it were up to him, the NPC section of Lair of the Hidden would never mention a Ruthven as the progenitor of such line (thanks Sarah or Janet for that!).<br /> <br /> -Saga</div> </div> </div> </div>It's indeed a possibility.<br /> But from my point of view there is really only one source that claims Lambach as a 4th generation cainite; <i>Clanbook Tzimisce</i>.<br /> If you go with <i>Lair of the Hidden</i>, I think it's odd that Lambach is mentioned as a cousin to Danika, if he is indeed the progenitor of the line that's only called &quot;Ruthven&quot; in Danika's family tree.<br /> <br /> <br /> Great input fellas. I really enjoy the continued discussion <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T05:06:01-07:00","id":500162,"title":"","content":"So I must admit I haven't given the Ruthvens much thought the last year, until recently stumbling upon Nevesa Zekistrya in the Hunters Hunted II.<br /> Apparently she can trace her lineage back to Lambach Ruthven, and thus being a descendant but not sharing the Ruthven family name. There are a lot of stuff that's pretty weird in the section where she's mentioned which I will not go into here, but what captured my attention was Nevesa being presented as a Old Clan Tzimisce.<br /> That may not be controversial to some of you, I don't know, but for me it started the slow realization that I really don't know just how the Old Clan Tzimisce works. Does this Nevesa being a Old Clan means Lambach is too, since the share the same blood? Or is it more like a sect allegiance? Or something else?<br /> <b>Just what kind of bloodline are the Old Clan Tzimisce? </b><br /> <br /> So I realize that the Old Clan has been its fair share of revisions since Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand, that they are presented by a host of different authors through different editions and so on.<br /> But still there must be a way to find some consensus in the material.<br /> I collected the material I could find below. So if your up for it, take a look at it, tell me if I missed any, and let me know how you treat the Old Clan ^^<br /> <br /> <b>So are the Old Clan Tzimisce a bloodline similar to the the Tlacique?</b><br /> By this I mean that they are a bloodline that split from the parent Clan a long, long time ago in matter of philosophy but more importantly in Disciplines shared through the blood. They have one or several progenitors that continue the bloodline from sire to childe.<br /> That all OCT have a shared trait in Dominate (according to character creation at least), that several sources take note that OCT is a ancient bloodline, and that it seems really clear to all Tzimisce that the OCT is a separate body may speak for this view.<br /> This view complicates a descendant of Lambach being OCT, since Lambach, Dracula and the rest of the family tree is presented as regular Tzimisce.<br /> Also, if you look at Kazimir Savostin's write up in Caine's Chosen, his sire seems pretty damn Old Clan including banning any use of Vicissitude, but Kazimir learns it and is presented as a regular Tzimsce.<br /> Sources supporting this might include: <i>The Hunters Hunted II, V20, CB Tzimisce, Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand, Lore of the Clans preview</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Or are the Old Clan Tzimisce a bloodline similar to the Serpents of the Light?</b><br /> By this I mean that they are a bloodline of ideology first and foremost. They are a coven of members that don't need to be relatives in the blood. A member of the parent Clan is considered to have joined the bloodline when joining (or leaving) the defining vampiric sect of the parent clan. It may also have some ties to geographical area.<br /> Speaking for this view is the sources mentioning the Anarch Revolt as a dividing factor between the Tzimisce and the OCT, like the rest of the antitribu factions of the clans. There are also sources stressing that the OCT is not a bloodline proper, but a small eastern European cult, and sources that say that the OCT usually(!) favors Dominate for Vicissitude \u2013 not having it as the defining factor.<br /> With this view Nevesa can be a OCT and yet having regular Tzimisce in the family tree.<br /> However, Danika Ruthven or Dracula not being a member of the Sabbat for ages should be considered OCT with this definition.<br /> Sources supporting this might include: <i>Encyclopaedia Vampirica, Guide to the Sabbat, Storytellers Handbook, Storytellers Guide to the Sabbat</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Or are the Old Clan Tzimisce a bloodline similar to the Warrior Setites?</b><br /> By this I mean that any Tzimisce can join the bloodline out of choice, and that choice will define the blood of themselves and future childer. The Old Clan chose not use their clan discipline of Vicissitude, and magically they get Dominate instead.<br /> This is more of a compromise suggestion. Speaking for this is that OCT-character creation describes Dominate in-clan, meaning they all have it, but yet it is not something carried within a collective lineage, being a cult or a gang of associates. There are also sources mentioning the choice not to use Vicissitude.<br /> With this view Nevesa or her sire, can have chosen to join the OCT, leaving the larger Tzimisce/Ruthven lineage.<br /> If this is the case, it is badly explained just how a group like the OCT (or the Warrior Setites for that matter) just can decide they want a new Clan Discipline.<br /> Sources supporting this might include: <i>Encyclopaedia Vampirica, V20, CB Tzimisce, Transylvania by Night, Lore of the Clans preview</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>But surely the Old Clan Tzimisce are not similar to the True Brujah?</b><br /> By this I mean that it's been established that the OCT is not the original Clan, while the rest of the Tzimisce with the antediluvian itself have been infected and warped into something else. It was retconned away as the delusions of a few OCT in Storytellers Handbook.<br /> Sources supporting this is basically <i>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand</i>, even though <i>V20</i> mentions the OCT predating fleshcrafting.<br /> Still, this view we've repeatedly been told is a myth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>So any thoughts?</b><br /> I'll post my sources used in the thread below.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T05:07:58-07:00","id":500186,"title":"","content":"\u201cHer real name is Nevesa Zekistrya, and she is a Tzimisce claiming to be a Caitiff. To be specific, she descends not from the modern metamorphs of the Sabbat, but from the Old Clan, those who did not acquire Vicissitude. She can trace her lineage back to Lambach Ruthven, though she is only a little over fifty years embraced. Nevesa is a powerful creature, despite her high Generation (9<sup>th</sup>)...\u201d<br /> - The Hunters Hunted 2 , page 150<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cTzimisce, Old World : A term used to describe some of the very old Tzimisce who remained in Europe and are nominally a part of the Sabbat. In fact, a very few of the chose to involve themselves in the politics of the sect at all, rather preferring to to lead their unlives as they wish. A small group within the Old World Tzimisce is obsessed with the idea that Vicissitude is in some way tainted or corrupted. These Old World Tzimisce refrain from employing the Discipline and prefer to cultivate Dominate.\u201c<br /> - Encyclopaedia Vampirica, page 176<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cOld World Tzimisce.<br /> Not every Tzimisce supports the Sabbat with her whole heart. Indeed when it comes to the arcane Tzimisce of the Old World, few supports it at all. Of course aligning oneself against the Sabbat is a good way to end up facing a down a pack of self-important rabble who want to strike at the elders for the good of the Sabbat and all such rubbish, so it's often easier to join the society yet abstain from its meetings.\u201d<br /> - Guide to the Sabbat, page 39<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cThe Old Clan Tzimisce are a small group of Fiends who predate the art of fleshcrafting. They regard Vicissitude as a disease of the soul, and refuse to learn or employ it. [\u2026] most members of the surviving members of this 'bloodline' still reside in what is now Romania, they govern their territories using Old World methods and customs.<br /> [\u2026]<br /> The Old Clan would prefer never to deal with the 'defiled' members of the Clan Tzimisce, but family is family and they can't survive the modern nights with no support. They make treaties and trade favors with other Fiends, sometimes lending support to the Sabbat as a whole in exchange for help and support when they need.\u201d<br /> - V20, page 439<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cThe Old Clan isn't a single bloodline but a collection of elders who agreed that the main body of the clan had deluded itself. A few believed (or claimed to) that Vicissitude wasn't a Discipline but a disease. Most Old Clan Tzimisce rejected that claim and focused instead on the obvious facts of insubordination. The Old Clan blamed the main clan's elders for allowing such a catastrophically successful revolt and concentrated on building an endless succession of schemes to reclaim the clan homelands for themselves.\u201d<br /> - Storytellers Handbook, page 181<br /> <br /> \u201cThe Old Clan Tzimisce \u2013 for no such thing as a Tzimisce antitribu exists- include both fairly disgruntled elders (and their well-indoctrinated, loyal childer) and a handful outright lunatics. [...]The Old Clan differs from the main Tzimisce clan in part because none of the Old Clan possess Vicissitude. [\u2026] Vicissitude strikes the Old Clan as unnatural and largely unwholesome, a perennial temptation to lose control and do something weird that provokes hostile response. The Old Clan Tzimisce fancy themselves as leaders steeped in wisdom and knowledge, not constantly mutating freak shows. The lunatic fringe takes this dislike of Vicissitude even further and claims that the Discipline isn't a Discipline at all but the manifestation of an evil spirit. [\u2026] Some members of the Old Clan never gave up their ancestral havens in Eastern Europe and now spend all (or nearly all) of their time at home, reviving the old ways as koldun and driving out other vampires. Old Clan Tzimisce without prior claims in Eastern Europe acquire their own estates [\u2026]\u201d<br /> - Storytellers Handbook, pages 191-192<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cDoes this branch of the Clan cling to the oldest ways of the Tzimisce, or are they something akin to puritan radicals? Accounts conflict, but at some point in the ancient past, a segment of the clan either refused or denounced the 'defilement' of Vicissitude. Some members of the Old Clan is exceedingly ancient [\u2026] these 'Old World' or 'Old Clan' reject the fleshcrafting arts [\u2026]<br /> Old Clan Tzimisce have mastered Caine's gift of mental domination in place of Vicissitude.\u201d<br /> - Clanbook Tzimisce revised, page 45<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cIt should be noted that not all ancient Tzimisce shared the same beliefs. One isolated sect, for instance, never accepted the practice of Vicissitude. Fleshcrafting was a method of tainting the soul to them. Centuries later, their descendants were eager to follow the philosophies of the feudal Tzimisce, yet they still preached against the dangers of body alteration. They typically distinguished themselves from their contemporaries by displaying curious anachronisms in attire and attitude. For this reason, they were mockingly dubbed the 'Old Clan Tzimisce' . Even after Transylvania ceased to be a feudal country, this small shrill sect still insisted on the old tradition of overseeing huge domains. As their isolationism and fanaticism increased, they manufactured elaborate legends to justify their extreme orthodoxy, such as the ludicrous tale of alien viruses infecting the rest of the clan.\u201d<br /> - Transylvania by Night, page 110<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cThe Tzimisce uninfected by Vicissitude serve the Black Hand faithfully. Despite the lack of this Discipline, Old Clan Tzimisce is still one of the oddest of all bloodlines and has very few members.\u201d<br /> - Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand, page 44<br /> <br /> \u201cThey are so ancient that even the oldest legends speak only of their childer. They are the Old Clan, the True Tzimisce, and all the Tzimisce of the Sabbat (and even the few within the Inconnu and the Camarilla) are the betrayers. Their childer abandoned the lineage of the Tzimisce and embrace the power of Vicissitude; their ways of anarchy brought ruin and death to both the innocent and the guilty of the clan. There are very few Old Clan Tzimisce, and each has sired only a few progeny.\u201d<br /> - Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand, page 62<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cOthers of the 'Old Clan', as the Sabbat call the Tzimisces outside the sect, are Inconnu. A small number have even been known to travel abroad, though this is rare. There is no plan by the 'Old Clan' to wage war on the young of their clan since the stakes would be so high. However, they seldom pass up an opportunity to slay a Sabbat Tzimisce as long as the danger is not too great. They know very little about the Sabbat, other than the fact that it is a vampire sect founded by their own clan's traitors.\u201c<br /> - Storytellers Guide to the Sabbat, pages 21-22<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201cAt an indeterminate point in our history, prodigals among the ancient line rejected the way of metamorphosis. They avoided all use of the fleshcrafting art, and even claim to have predated it. They called temselves the 'Pure Clan' or the 'Old Clan', depending on translation.<br /> One story says that they discovered the Discipline of mutable flesh was itself a sentient disease, a demonic, possessing force from some incomprehensible dimension.\u201d<br /> - Lore of the Clans preview, Tzimisce section<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T14:28:16-07:00","id":500780,"title":"","content":"At the very least, the idea from <i>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand</i> that the Old Clan diverged as a bloodline before the clan developed Vicissitude is bunk. Kartarirya, Demdemeh, and Yorak all demonstrably practiced that discipline at high levels, and one can hardly suppose an entire group of 4th generation elders predating <b>them</b>. Vicissitude likely originated with the Eldest Fiend itself before it embraced any progeny.<br /> <br /> The White Wolf Wikia lists Dracula's clan as Old Clan Tzimisce, and certainly the thematic idea of them can be traced to Dracula in real-world literature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T14:40:40-07:00","id":500789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=500780#post500780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the very least, the idea from <i>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand</i> that the Old Clan diverged as a bloodline before the clan developed Vicissitude is bunk. Kartarirya, Demdemeh, and Yorak all demonstrably practiced that discipline at high levels, and one can hardly suppose an entire group of 4th generation elders predating <b>them</b>. Vicissitude likely originated with the Eldest Fiend itself before it embraced any progeny.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=500780#post500780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The White Wolf Wikia lists Dracula's clan as Old Clan Tzimisce, and certainly the thematic idea of them can be traced to Dracula in real-world literature.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, Dracula is more of the grim aristocrat than fleshcrafting savage, but we have published stats for him in at least two different books, both saying he's a Tzimisce (and having Vicissitude).<br /> I'm going to need another source than the wiki.<br /> <br /> But to matter of hand, how would you describe the Old Clan as a bloodline?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T21:52:41-07:00","id":501354,"title":"","content":"The Wiki does show him as possessing Vicissitude as well, but appears to have been changed back to basic Tzimisce in the last week or so. Then again published books show a member of the Oradea League who not only possesses Vicissitude, it's his highest discipline (at <i><b>8 dots</b></i>!), and that group is specifically indentified as being Old Clan. The stats have never matched the fluff regarding the Old Clan's refusal to learn the discipline.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-03T00:26:29-07:00","id":501457,"title":"","content":"I guess your referring to Hedeon Yaroslavish of the Oradea League? He's from an time when Humanity 0 was considered totally ok to scheme away eternity with <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> But in all seriousness, Clanbook Tzimisce revised is quite clear in separating the Oradea League and the Old Clan Tzimisce as separate things. That all Oradea League members are Old Clan is a thing from the Storytellers Guide to the Sabbat, but there &quot;Old Clan&quot; is used as a moniker for &quot;not Sabbat&quot;.<br /> Yet the wiki claims with all certainty that the Oradea League is all Old Clan - that's why I'm not debating the white wolf wiki, and asking for real hard sources.<br /> <br /> I treat Hedeon and Dracula as regular tzimisce until I can see clear indications of the contrary, but I'd love to hear your arguments.<br /> In fact, the only clearly defined Old Clan Tzimsice I can find in the books is Jaromir Cerny from Children of the Revolution (who is evidently short on Vicissitude).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-03T10:00:32-07:00","id":501817,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I can't wait to see how the old clan will be portrayed in the new tal'mahe'ra book.<br /> <br /> <br /> But the assumption that the Ruthven were old clan from the start doesn't make much sense, as the eldest and its childer all practice vicissitude. The old clan could have emerged at a later period, refusing to practice fleshcrafting, but they cannot be the 'original' clan.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also, regarding the eldest, the creature passed through at least one rebirth, and might not be the same entity after it: it was killed by the Salubri warrior Samiel, and was rebirthed by the Dracon and nurtured back to health by Yorak at the carpathians, after wich Koldunism flourished in the clan. <br /> <br /> <br /> Might this be the time the old clan emerged? It was a time when there was technically no eldest, the greatest fleshcrafter. Yorak's attention would likewise be focused on the eldest.<br /> <br /> <br /> Sadly, there is no date for when this happened, but Salubri lore tells about some baali wars against the gnostics during early christianity that was already fought without Samiel. So the death of both Samiel and the eldest would have to predate christian era.<br /> <br /> <br /> I can see the Ruthven line falling out of favor then, as protectors of the eldest's resting place. They couldn't protect the eldest from Samiel, and weren't chosen to be its guardians after its rebirth, being replaced by Yorak.<br /> <br /> <br /> Later, before the rise of feudalism, maybe even before the roman conquest of Transylvania, somehow the Ruthvens were once again guardians of the eldest (assuming they did indeed fell from grace because of Samiel's attack). <br /> <br /> However, the rebirth of the eldest might have happened closer to truly ancient times, as Samiel was last truly active in the second city and the first baali wars. <br /> <br /> Actually, considering how old the rebirth of the eldest, and a possible appropriate time for the emergence of the old clan, the Ruthven family might come after, effectively becoming the eldest guardians after it was nursed by Yorak. This would explain how many of their numbers have an affinity for Koldunism. They could even have become as old clan, assuming that was a trend of rejecting vicissitude that started when the eldest 'died', but became fleshcrafters after the eldest regained influence over the clan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":412,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","user":"valismedsen","date":". 05-03-2015, 01:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-04T02:33:15-07:00","id":502661,"title":"","content":"I like the upcoming V20 Lore of the Clans explanation.<br /> Namely that the Old Clan Tzimisce reject Vicissitude not out of fear of some alien space parasite but out of fear of the Tzimisce Antediluvian.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5520-hideamuro","name":"Hideamuro","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-04T09:04:27-07:00","id":502990,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hideamuro</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=502661#post502661\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the upcoming V20 Lore of the Clans explanation.<br /> Namely that the Old Clan Tzimisce reject Vicissitude not out of fear of some alien space parasite but out of fear of the Tzimisce Antediluvian.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for replaying <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> Yea, alien parasites is the biggest no no, but still; these antediluvian-worshipping cultists of the Manus Nigrum fearing the Tzimisce antediluvian possessing them? Wouldn't that be more like the ultimate honor, becoming one with one of their gods?<br /> <br /> But never mind, my point from above is more of me asking what exactly does &quot;rejecting Vicissitude&quot; mean?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>valismedsen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=501817#post501817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I can't wait to see how the old clan will be portrayed in the new tal'mahe'ra book.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hehe, concerning the new new Tal'mahe'ra book I'm feeling both excited as hell and scared shitless at the same time <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> Let's hope that the final answer on the nature of the Old Clan Tzimisce is in there.<br /> <br /> I love your idea that with the antediluvian weak after It's rebirth and Yorak nursing it, the two greatest fleshcrafters was taken out of the picture and perhaps opening up a window of opportunity for the OCT. As master kolduns themselves, or some of them at least, maybe they later spiritually encouraged Yorak to pursue his councel with Kupala and him drifting out of the affairs of the feudal clan? Just a thought.<br /> You presented a really neat theory about some of the main clan noticing the &quot;failure of Vicissitude&quot; with the death of the Eldest, and those thoughts again becoming unpopular with the Eldest's re-emergence, and I especially liked how you included the Rutvens within. I always enjoy reading about your ideas and I hope you know that I very much respect you Bruno.<br /> But again what does rejecting Vicissitude mean?<br /> - &quot;I don't want to use Vicissitude, it's gross.&quot;<br /> - &quot;Fine&quot; Poff! &quot;Here's Dominate instead&quot;<br /> I really would like to know how you would explain the OCT as a bloodline.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-04T11:54:59-07:00","id":503214,"title":"","content":"The authors chose their words carefully, keeping the Old Clan very vague. From the Revised Clanbook I get the feeling that it's more a matter of choice to belong to the Old Clan than it is a Bloodline united by the same blood and powers. I would compare them to Angellis Ater which I personally think is more a choice to belong to than being born into. They are still Lasombra, just with a very different agenda and politics than most Lasombra, making them an extreme faction within the Lasombra Clan. I would say that the same goes for Old Clan Tzimisce. They are under the title Factions with other, lets say \u201dreal Tzimisce\u201d groups. But also in the text about the Old Clan on page 45 the author writes to Methuselah this: \u201dThe Old Clan still practices the ceremonies of coronation and rites-of-passage to childer. The ritae are abhorrent to them. Perhaps you would find more identifiable company among their ranks, old one\u201d. Now that sentence could be read in two different ways, that's why I say that the authors chose their words carefully. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It could be read in a way where the author suggests that among the Old Clan the ancient one could find vampires that still share many of the ideas and customs from when the old one was active last time and speaking with them might please him. Or it could be read like a suggestion that maybe he should join their ranks for the same reasons since he would belong better amongst them. If you read it like the latter then that would suggest that it is a matter of choice since Methuselah is confirmed \u201dreal\u201d Tzimisce.<br /> I don't own the Gangrel Clanbook or the Brujah Clanbook, but in the Revised Clanbooks I do own, all Faction sections include more \u201da-matter-of-choice-to-belong\u201d-factions than pure Bloodlines. So if you check the Brujah Clanbook or the Gangrel Clanbook it could be interesting to see how they adress Anda or True Brujah which we know is pure Bloodlines. I checked the Setite Clanbook and they used a different title than Factions there, they called it Setite Divisions, also notice how they split them up a bit, seperating The Chort of Wepwawet, The Scandinavian Setites, The sisterhood of Sekhmet and the Cult of Typhon Trismegistus from Children of Damballah, Serpents of the Light, Daitya, Tlacique and the Warrior Setites.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/629-anda","name":"anda","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"156"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-04T14:14:10-07:00","id":503479,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the compliments, original_anarch!<br /> <br /> In my games a player character from the Old Clan has indeed become a default Tzimisce, by becoming &quot;infected&quot; with Vicissitude. Since the moment the character learned the Discipline, I began treating her as a normal Tzimisce, with Vicissitude replacing Dominate (however, this particular character never practiced Dominate, so the point was moot).<br /> <br /> I also implement a system where a player can exchange a dot from any Virtue, the Path or Willpower, or a Derangement, for a dot in Vicissitude (once the character has tasted the blood with someone with Vicissitude, or is a Tzimisce). This is a house rule inspired by the table in Guide to the Sabbat that offers some rules for learning Vicissitude at different XP costs to reflect the mutable nature of the discipline (or rates of infection, depending on how you see it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-04T22:10:02-07:00","id":503820,"title":"","content":"I prefer to think of the Old Clan as a group of old, traditional Tzimisce who are different from the main clan in outlook and chosen methodology rather than blood (basically, the 2nd edition approach). The oldest among them probably had Vicissitude as a clan discipline but chose to concentrate on developing Dominate instead (as well as being the main practitioners of Koldunic Sorcery), while younger ones might have Dominate as a clan discipline because that's what their sires focused on when training them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-05T07:44:19-07:00","id":504134,"title":"","content":"I do enjoy these speculative threads! :-)<br /> <br /> I do not see any DA supplements as your sources. Whether to include them into your research is of course up to you. However, the following is written about the Dracul in the upcoming VD20 Rulebook (It could change since the final Errata has not been released yet):<br /> <br /> \u201c<br /> The name \u201cfiend\u201d is an insult. We were the Dragons of old. We shall be again.<br /> <br /> Most do not realize that Clan Tzimisce is a clan split along old generational lines. Deep in the mountains of Transylvania and along the periphery of all Tzimisce lands, are those who call themselves Dracul, the first of their clan. Once they were the only Tzimisce, and until as recently as a few centuries ago, they retained a superiority of numbers. But power shifts as times change, and they have been relegated to the minority. They still possess significant numbers, but their power is on the wane.<br /> <br /> The principle difference between Dracul Tzimisce and all the rest is that the Dracul predate the use of Vicissitude. In the manner of crotchety old men, they regard fleshcrafting as a debasement of the clan, a showy bag of tricks that betray the Tzimisce\u2019s destiny as sovereigns by gutting them of powers more complimentary to that task. Still, the tide of progress is against them, and some few have begun to realize that struggling against it may mean drowning in a sea of enemies.<br /> <br /> Nickname: Dragons. Some among younger generation Tzimisce have taken to calling them the Old Clan, always in a disparaging tone.<br /> <br /> Disciplines: Animalism, Auspex, Dominate<br /> <br /> Weakness: The Dracul suffer the same weakness as all other Tzimisce.<br /> \u201c<br /> - VDA20 page 453<br /> <br /> /Ho Dracon","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/390-ho-dracon","name":"Ho Dracon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar390_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-05T12:29:56-07:00","id":504491,"title":"","content":"<i>Sigh</i>. There's a reason I almost never quote DA sources. If there are two possible interpretations of any factoid, historical occurrence, or bit of mechanics, the VDA20 authors invariably go with the one that's less logical and more annoying to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-05T12:40:36-07:00","id":504496,"title":"","content":"Thanks for replaying and participating all <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I'll try to adress your points, but I'm probably going to forget things.<br /> It's really interesting seeing you, like the authors, taking different approaches to the Old Clan.<br /> <br /> @Anda<br /> I really agree that most mentions of the OCT is very vague. You're writing that &quot;you get the feeling that...&quot;, and I believe that's how most of us react to the material - responding positively to a particular phrasing, or similar, and getting a subtle push into choosing what to believe.<br /> Methuselah from CB:Tzimisce is such a interesting character. Maybe he would have more in common with the classic lords of the Old Clan, rather than the fire-dancing Sabbat, (and yet they made \u201cthe Sabbat's cause sang through his veins\u201d). And at the other side of coin; he probably would have more in common with the likes of golden oldies like Lambach or Velya then any raving Manus Nigrum-cultist ranting of alien parasites disguising as vampires.<br /> Clanbook Brujah separates the rough social labels of iconoclasts and idealists from what they call actual bloodlines. In the later section they do have the True Brujah, but also the Brujah antitribu (who I would call a social diversion from the main Clan).<br /> Clanbook Gangrel separates their antitribu and anarchs from true diversions of the blood like Anda or Ahrimanes.<br /> I'm going to assign you to the \u201cSerpents of the Light\u201d camp, if that's alright with you? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> @Valismedsen<br /> Your take sounds similar to how I suspect is the case with Kazimir Savostin. With \u201creplacing\u201d, do you mean as Clan Discipline or the actual dots?<br /> Am I understanding you correctly if I assign you to the \u201cWarrior Setite\u201d camp? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> @Matt the Bruins fan<br /> I really forgot to thanks you for your responses above.<br /> You mean that they started out as a social bloodline, that turned into a blood-bloodline right?<br /> Kind of like the Tlacique most probably? Or do you reckon they're they still accepting converts?<br /> <br /> @Ho Dracon<br /> I do too, I really do <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> To be perfectly honest I couldn't recall seeing the Old Clan in the Dark Ages line at all, except in Transylvania by Night. I roughly browsed Guide to the High Clans and the Road books without luck, and my Dark Ages Europe copy is elsewhere but at home. <br /> In fact, the absence of OCT in the Dark Ages material almost had me believe that the where no OCT recognized as a separate body before the Anarch Revolt. <br /> Do you have any suggestion in which DA books the OCT are presented?<br /> <br /> Thanks for the part from the VD20. I thought I looked for the OCT there but I must have missed it, or I looked in the wrong place. <br /> I like that it could kind of fit with Valis' thoughts above, but as many have pointed out in this thread the part with the OCT predating fleshcrafting is quite problematic. <br /> How do you personally look at it Ho Dracon?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-05T12:58:12-07:00","id":504526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You mean that they started out as a social bloodline, that turned into a blood-bloodline right?<br /> Kind of like the Tlacique most probably? Or do you reckon they're they still accepting converts? </div> </div> </div>Right. I would imagine they still occasionally accept converts who are committed to either their dread-lord-ruling-from-the-ruined-castle aesthetic or to being students of koldunism. But it's probably a lot rarer to go that route than to be trained from the ground up as a fledgeling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-06T08:53:29-07:00","id":505636,"title":"","content":"First off great thread! Love it!<br /> <br /> Little late to the party. I might suggest giving the ruthven revenant line if you go that way Protean as one of the inclans. <br /> <br /> Simply because lambach is described as using the elder Protean power turn about in new York by night. Usually elder plus powers are in clan. Which is why I suggest it.<br /> <br /> Just a thought.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3215-supersabbatst","name":"SuperSabbatST","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3215_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"196"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-06T12:35:41-07:00","id":505969,"title":"","content":"@original_anarch<br /> <br /> As Clan Discipline, not dots. So yeah, Warrior Setite (and Ahirimanes and Children of Osiris and Malkavians going Dementation). Something happens (a ritual, a unlife refocus, an unlife-changing experience) and the blood changes and the vampire is now a bloodline member instead of a mainstream Clan, or a mainstream Clan instead of a mere bloodline member. This would be rare, though.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-06T14:01:56-07:00","id":506042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>original_anarch</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=504496#post504496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> @Ho Dracon<br /> I do too, I really do <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> To be perfectly honest I couldn't recall seeing the Old Clan in the Dark Ages line at all, except in Transylvania by Night. I roughly browsed Guide to the High Clans and the Road books without luck, and my Dark Ages Europe copy is elsewhere but at home.<br /> In fact, the absence of OCT in the Dark Ages material almost had me believe that the where no OCT recognized as a separate body before the Anarch Revolt.<br /> Do you have any suggestion in which DA books the OCT are presented?<br /> <br /> Thanks for the part from the VD20. I thought I looked for the OCT there but I must have missed it, or I looked in the wrong place.<br /> I like that it could kind of fit with Valis' thoughts above, but as many have pointed out in this thread the part with the OCT predating fleshcrafting is quite problematic.<br /> How do you personally look at it Ho Dracon?</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I haven't looked further than TbN myself either before with regards to OCT. OCT is an oddity for sure. I have only ST'd in Dark Ages, and from my point of view the Old Country has no room for the Old Clan in that era. I made a quite extensive work to populate Transylvania using the available sources before starting my TC campaign last year, and there is a large number of named voivodes in the sources. Large enough to fill up all the tirsas in Transylvania proper and not one is mentioned being OCT.<br /> <br /> I might be an anomaly at this forum, since I haven't ST'd VtM. I base my entire world around the material found in VDA, DAV and the upcoming VDA20. I consider VtM material secondary sources (at least until the campaign moves in to the years covered in Victorian Age and Final Nights). So, being a purist in this way, the qoute I made about the Dracul bloodline is considered &quot;the truth&quot;, and other interpretations will be &quot;heresies&quot;. However, since I have already established (to my players) that the Dracon is the oldest surviving childe of the Eldest, the OCT need to be decendants from older, destroyed 4th gens. Perhaps there are some ancient 5th gen OCT's and their decendants lurking in the northern parts of the Carpathian range (northwest of Transylvania), waiting for an opportunity to return to the forefront once the rest of the clan implodes in the Anarch Revolt.<br /> <br /> /Ho Dracon","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/390-ho-dracon","name":"Ho Dracon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar390_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T11:45:52-07:00","id":507050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuperSabbatST</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=505636#post505636\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First off great thread! Love it!<br /> <br /> Little late to the party. I might suggest giving the ruthven revenant line if you go that way Protean as one of the inclans.<br /> <br /> Simply because lambach is described as using the elder Protean power turn about in new York by night. Usually elder plus powers are in clan. Which is why I suggest it.<br /> <br /> Just a thought.</div> </div> </div> </div>Glad you enjoyed it.<br /> Protean could tie the Ruthvens even stronger to the Basarab revenant family. Dracula have some dots in the Discipline too, while Danika and Anastazi don't.<br /> But I'm curious where exactly it says that Lambach used the Protean effect you suggest, I couldn't find it at first glance.<br /> <br /> <br /> @Anda, Matt the Bruins fan, Valismedsen and Ho Dracon<br /> <br /> I thinks it cool that you all take different approaches to the sources, and that you all reach different conclusions about the nature of the Old Clan Tzimisce. It means I wasn't totally off, when I confessed just not getting them in bloodline terms <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> As for adhering to the DA material while playing DA games, I thinks it sounds natural. In the past I recall seeing more discussions weather or not the Masquerade and the Dark Ages was taking place in the same world at all (discussions that's still taking place about the other WOD brands).<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T12:16:15-07:00","id":507088,"title":"","content":"To play Devil's advocate, the fifth dot of Visceratika in V20 theoretically allows one to do what Lambach Ruthven was observed doing in <i>New York by Night</i>. While a Tzimisce elder is probably more likely to possess Protean at high levels than that discipline, it is a possibility. As is Lambach having done the work to translate the ritual the Gargoyle power is based on into one usable via Koldunic Sorcery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-08T23:46:35-07:00","id":508801,"title":"","content":"Cause the specific qoute was requested :-)<br /> <br /> New York by Night page 36<br /> <br /> &quot;They fought like dervishes, Polonia's sword taking off heads whenever it left its scabbard and Ruthven sinking into the ground before you only to appear behind you with his fangs in your spine&quot;<br /> <br /> That with the Guide to the Anarch level 7 Protean power &quot;Turnabout&quot;, is what made me thing he clearly had level 7 Protean and usually (but not always) the NPC sheet's elder plus powers are in clan. Hence Lambach might indeed have Protean in clan. Which does work with the Basrab theory.<br /> <br /> Just a thought :-)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3215-supersabbatst","name":"SuperSabbatST","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3215_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"196"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-11T12:10:19-07:00","id":511020,"title":"","content":"Gracias SuperSabbatST, I totally looked for the quote at the wrong place!<br /> That the Turnabout power is especially described as a power originally used by the Eastern European Gangrel and Tzimisce during the Anarch Revolt makes it a really, really nice fit for Lambach. Even though, as Matt the Bruins fan mentions, it theoretically can be Visceratica or something else entirely (even artistic expression - it's not like Polonia's sword decapitated someone <i>every single time)</i>.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/82-original_anarch","name":"original_anarch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar82_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-27T07:11:14-08:00","id":1292478,"title":"","content":"When I read this thread I got an idea, I want to share:<br /> What if Lambach Ruthven is a disguise for Ionache, the 4th gen methuselah (PGtHC) and contemporary of Yorak and the Dracon, who was allegedly responsible for the construction of the Tzimisce Voivodate during the early middle ages and held the office of Voivode of Voivodes for the first time?<br /> I came up with this idea for the following reasons:<br /> 1) Ionache disappeared right around the time Lambach was allegedly embraced (1000 CE), if I unterstand your research correctly. He seems to be quite forgotten around 1230, which seams a bit weird given his deeds and age.<br /> 2) Lambachs rivalry with Triglav over the Embrace of Shaagra would be more understandable if he was in fact Ionache, whose construction of the Voivodate would nicely fit to the search for new-Leadership that marked Shaagras embrace (Cb-Tz-Rev).<br /> 3) The family name Ruthven, that &quot;Lord Ruthven&quot; as their founder (as LotH put it) would indicate, always seemed a bit anachronistic to me for a medieaval or even older dynasty.<br /> I like the idea of Lambach as decadent and cowardly, which doesn't sit well with the architect of the Voivodate, I know. But maybe his disappearance was due to an event that changed him/his actions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25099-athanasius","name":"Athanasius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-02T21:54:51-08:00","id":1293417,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Athanasius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=1292478#post1292478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I read this thread I got an idea, I want to share:<br /> What if Lambach Ruthven is a disguise for Ionache, the 4th gen methuselah (PGtHC) and contemporary of Yorak and the Dracon, who was allegedly responsible for the construction of the Tzimisce Voivodate during the early middle ages and held the office of Voivode of Voivodes for the first time?<br /> I came up with this idea for the following reasons:<br /> 1) Ionache disappeared right around the time Lambach was allegedly embraced (1000 CE), if I unterstand your research correctly. He seems to be quite forgotten around 1230, which seams a bit weird given his deeds and age.<br /> 2) Lambachs rivalry with Triglav over the Embrace of Shaagra would be more understandable if he was in fact Ionache, whose construction of the Voivodate would nicely fit to the search for new-Leadership that marked Shaagras embrace (Cb-Tz-Rev).<br /> 3) The family name Ruthven, that &amp;quot;Lord Ruthven&amp;quot; as their founder (as LotH put it) would indicate, always seemed a bit anachronistic to me for a medieaval or even older dynasty.<br /> I like the idea of Lambach as decadent and cowardly, which doesn't sit well with the architect of the Voivodate, I know. But maybe his disappearance was due to an event that changed him/his actions?</div> </div> </div> </div>I thought of that one when DA revised was released, and yes, it might be possible.<br /> <br /> Cb-TzR (IIRC) stated that Lambach was embraced before current organized religions, therefore, he is old. Then, the Ruthven line was left in charge of guarding the nascent Antediluvian (given to them by the Dracon after he was destroyed by Samiel) in Sarmizegetuza.<br /> <br /> Being the chronicler of the Eldest, founder of the Voivodate, and guardian of the &quot;body&quot; of the Eldest would make sense. But yet, I believe that Ionache and Lambach are different cainites.<br /> <br /> - Saga<br /> <br /> PD: I forgot how much I enjoyed that thread in the old forums!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","name":"Saga","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-03T03:51:11-08:00","id":1293442,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saga</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=1293417#post1293417\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Cb-TzR (IIRC) stated that Lambach was embraced before current organized religions, therefore, he is old.</div> </div> </div> </div>Citing an in-game-source, one Devin and Alison Markbury of London (Cb-Tz-R, p. 19), yes. But these whole pages concerning Lambach seem to be more the attempt of some Sabbat-chroniclers to get Lambachs importance for the history of their sect in line with his importance to the clan or the founder as a whole.<br /> Lambach's known actions all (with the exception of his anger about Shaagra not being embraced by himself, told by an ingame-source as well) occur during or after the time of the Anarch Revolt (Kupala's Night, Lugojs Diablerie, Draculas Embrace), so, he is very important in the Sabbats founding days, therefore, thought and spoken out of their perspective, he &quot;has&quot; to be higher up the chain of the Tzimisce lineage and older or as old as the other big players of the time (Byelobog, Velya, Yorak...).<br /> <br /> I think I'll go with the Ionache-attempt, at least as a founder of the Ruthvens, and watch, were that leads me.<br /> <br /> But as I was writing: It has been hinted that the Eldest was disappointed of Yorak and Byelobog, and the Dracon was out of the picture by 1230, so maybe the Eldest chose one of his &quot;younger&quot; childer to become his chronicler/watcher of events?<br /> <br /> But I still don't see why it should be Lambach, of all people. Because &quot;he is a survivor&quot;? What did he survive, in fact, predating Lugojs Diablerie? The attack on his sire's (Tabak's) estate by the Anarchs?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25099-athanasius","name":"Athanasius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-03T04:43:28-08:00","id":1293447,"title":"","content":"Canon lore considering the Ruthvens is a hot mess.<br /> <br /> Clanbook Tzimisce Revised says Lambach was a 4th generation childe of the Antediluvian.<br /> <br /> Gehenna, Transylvania Chronicles, and at least a few other books say he was 5tth generation.<br /> <br /> Encyclopedia Vampirica calls him at 7th.<br /> <br /> Some sources say he was Tabak's childe (or was it sire?). Some say he was his broodmate. Beckett's Jyhad Diary calls into question whether he was even Vlad Tepes' sire at all.<br /> <br /> The idea that Lambach might have been another guise for Ionache is interesting. The latter's disappearance isn't explained anywhere.<br /> <br /> Ultimately though, I like Lambach as a coward. The setting has <i>plenty</i> of fiendish masterminds already. Lambach adds some needed variety and nicely illustrates how being of low generation isn't an inherent indicator of competence or badass-itude. Just luck. <br /> <br /> I also like the amount of contradictory information there is floating around about Lambach. Even for GMs who have a clear origin/generation in mind for him, misinformation is everywhere in the Jyhad and Kindred investigating the Tzimisce elder could be expected to get lost amidst that school of red herrings .","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-03T07:08:11-08:00","id":1293460,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Athanasius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=1293442#post1293442\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Citing an in-game-source, one Devin and Alison Markbury of London (Cb-Tz-R, p. 19), yes. But these whole pages concerning Lambach seem to be more the attempt of some Sabbat-chroniclers to get Lambachs importance for the history of their sect in line with his importance to the clan or the founder as a whole.<br /> Lambach's known actions all (with the exception of his anger about Shaagra not being embraced by himself, told by an ingame-source as well) occur during or after the time of the Anarch Revolt (Kupala's Night, Lugojs Diablerie, Draculas Embrace), so, he is very important in the Sabbats founding days, therefore, thought and spoken out of their perspective, he &amp;quot;has&amp;quot; to be higher up the chain of the Tzimisce lineage and older or as old as the other big players of the time (Byelobog, Velya, Yorak...).<br /> <br /> I think I'll go with the Ionache-attempt, at least as a founder of the Ruthvens, and watch, were that leads me.<br /> <br /> But as I was writing: It has been hinted that the Eldest was disappointed of Yorak and Byelobog, and the Dracon was out of the picture by 1230, so maybe the Eldest chose one of his &amp;quot;younger&amp;quot; childer to become his chronicler/watcher of events?<br /> <br /> But I still don't see why it should be Lambach, of all people. Because &amp;quot;he is a survivor&amp;quot;? What did he survive, in fact, predating Lugojs Diablerie? The attack on his sire's (Tabak's) estate by the Anarchs?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, he DID hinted on where to locate and formulate the ritual to break the bonds through Vaulderie and literally have every anarch have Tzimisce blood inside them. Beyond his fame, he has importance to the Eldest.<br /> <br /> - Saga","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","name":"Saga","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-03T08:05:23-08:00","id":1293472,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Athanasius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=1293442#post1293442\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Citing an in-game-source, one Devin and Alison Markbury of London (Cb-Tz-R, p. 19), yes. But these whole pages concerning Lambach seem to be more the attempt of some Sabbat-chroniclers to get Lambachs importance for the history of their sect in line with his importance to the clan or the founder as a whole.<br /> Lambach's known actions all (with the exception of his anger about Shaagra not being embraced by himself, told by an ingame-source as well) occur during or after the time of the Anarch Revolt (Kupala's Night, Lugojs Diablerie, Draculas Embrace), so, he is very important in the Sabbats founding days, therefore, thought and spoken out of their perspective, he &quot;has&quot; to be higher up the chain of the Tzimisce lineage and older or as old as the other big players of the time (Byelobog, Velya, Yorak...).</div> </div> </div> </div>Tzimisce clanbook has two sources about Lambach being old as dirt and being a 4th gen: the first is in-game-source, but the last is an out of character source were the writers explains the why of Lambach.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lambach\u2019s Purpose<br /> [Tzimisce] saw something in Lambach Ruthven<br /> to consider him worthy of Becoming. Lambach has<br /> reputedly seen the Antediluvian twice in recent<br /> times; once when Lugoj \u201cdrained\u201d it and a second<br /> time beneath New York\u2019s streets. Both times, he<br /> survived, even though he saw things he should not<br /> have. The question, however, remains: Why? Unbeknownst<br /> to anyone, including Lambach himself,<br /> he is the Eldest\u2019s chronicler. He is the eyes and ears<br /> of his master and documents the clan\u2019s actions while<br /> the Antediluvian remains in torpor. Occasionally,<br /> when danger is about to befall Lambach, the Eldest<br /> imbues him with a portion of its strength or power to<br /> allow him to escape and run. Lambach will survive<br /> until the end times; he will be the last Tzimisce to<br /> fall to the internal scourge of his sire\u2019s million-fold<br /> teeth. Until then, he is truly immortal; the Eldest<br /> will not let him perish </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I still don't see why it should be Lambach, of all people. Because &quot;he is a survivor&quot;? What did he survive, in fact, predating Lugojs Diablerie? The attack on his sire's (Tabak's) estate by the Anarchs?<br /> </div> </div> </div><br /> If we take a different point of view than Lambach is the apex of survivors in the World of Darkness.<br /> Think about it: how many Tzimisce 4th gen survived in to the modern nights? Yorak was consumed by the Chatedral, Byelobog was diablerized, Triglav died at the hands of his brother, Dracon...well...it's indisposed, Gallod died, Andaleon disappeared,Demdeh became something that is not human anymore, only Kartarirya managed to survive, but he is quite the recluse.<br /> <br /> Only Lambach is still alive and active in the modern nights: by being a &quot;weakling&quot;, by never becoming a threat to anyone he survived where none of his brothers managed. Even more Lambach never retreated from the world like his brothers (who were all recluse of somekind). Lambach adapted with ease. While all his brothers choose isolation he was a main character in the events of vampiric history. He survived the anarch revolt and was one of the main catalyst of the first vaulderie, he was there when Lugoj attacked the monastery of Sernog, he survived the ambush from Dracula and the Justicars, he managed to survive the siege of New York. He survived everything he faced, without hiding from the world like Yorak or Byelobog.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12142,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","user":"Undead rabbit","date":". 03-03-2019, 11:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","name":"Undead rabbit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-03T09:11:34-08:00","id":1293486,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saga</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/28828-continued-the-ruthvens?p=1293460#post1293460\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, he DID hinted on where to locate and formulate the ritual to break the bonds through Vaulderie and literally have every anarch have Tzimisce blood inside them. Beyond his fame, he has importance to the Eldest.<br /> <br /> - Saga</div> </div> </div> </div>That's another thing that bugs me, though. Maybe it's because my first &quot;meeting&quot; with Lambach was through TC2: In this chronicle (TC I - Dark Tides rising), it's the PCs that gather and use/bury the information of the founder's resting place, not Lambach. He enters the picture not for a while, actually, when he is needed for Draculas Embrace - if you don't use a PC for that, that is.<br /> I always felt a little bit as if Lambach was the NPC who did the stuff, that needed to be done, but could not be/should not be done by a mysterious &quot;coterie of Vampires&quot; (a phrase used to indicate player's actions at times). Or maybe it's vice versa and they gave some of Lambachs actions to players for the purpose of the Transylvania Chronicles, who knows?<br /> <br /> Concerning Cb-Tz-Rev, I saw, earlier today, the phrase &quot;Becoming&quot; which seems to mean &quot;Chosen for the Embrace&quot; like you said. Before I thought Lambach was just &quot;Chosen&quot;, but that &quot;Becoming&quot; would indeed be an author's hint on him being 4th gen.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25099-athanasius","name":"Athanasius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-04T18:01:13-08:00","id":1293931,"title":"","content":"I approve.<br /> <br /> -Ruthven","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24582-ruthven","name":"Ruthven","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-04T09:40:03-08:00","id":1431011,"title":"","content":"Found out that Ruthven is a name of Scottish origin. What about Transylvania, then?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3792-trueann","name":"trueann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3792_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}